  "diagnosis_text": "### Diagnosis\n\nThe bottleneck is **s2 port 1 (`s2-eth1`), toward s1**, on the path:\n\n`h1 \u2014 s4 \u2014 s2 \u2014 s1 \u2014 s3 \u2014 s6 \u2014 h5`\n\nEvidence:\n\n- `h1 \u2192 h5` TCP test was severely impaired: sender managed only about **0.35 Mbit/s**, and the receiver summary recorded no completed payload.\n- ICMP still worked, but RTT was abnormally high: **~103 ms average**, with no packet loss.\n- Control paths were fast:\n  - `h1 \u2192 h2` locally through s4: **38.7 Gbit/s**\n  - `h1 \u2192 h3` via s4\u2013s2\u2013s5: **34.2 Gbit/s**\n  - `h7 \u2192 h5` via s7\u2013s3\u2013s6: **32.8 Gbit/s**\n- `h3 \u2192 h5`, which also crosses s2\u2013s1\u2013s3, showed the same severe failure. This isolates the problem to the central path.\n- On **s2 port 1**, a two-second sample showed:\n  - transmit rate almost exactly **1.0 Mbit/s**\n  - **10 packets / 15,140 bytes queued**\n  - **364 queue overlimits**\n  - no interface errors or drops\n- The peer and downstream interfaces had no queue backlog or overlimits. All ports are enabled, and s2 port 1 advertises **10 Gbit/s**.\n\nThis strongly indicates an **egress traffic-control rate limit/shaper on `s2-eth1`**, rather than a failed link or ONOS connectivity issue. The next step is to inspect the Linux/OVS qdisc configuration on that interface, for example `tc -s qdisc show dev s2-eth1` and associated class/filter settings.",
